        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Verdict: Who Should Buy Semco Teak Sealer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy Semco if you own outdoor teak, on a boat or in a garden, and you want it to stay golden rather than grey without ever sanding or stripping it. It suits people who will spend an hour each spring, and ideally again in late summer, washing and wiping on a fresh coat. In that routine, its strengths line up: no lap marks, no film to fail, and a maintenance coat that needs no deep prep.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you want a set-and-forget finish, or a glossy, built-up look. It will not give the shine of varnish, and on horizontal teak in strong sun owners report needing more than one coat a season. If your teak is already silver and you are happy with that, you do not need any sealer at all; teak does not need a finish to last.
          </p>

          <p>
            For shade, the maker's own guidance is a good starting point: Natural for boat decks and trim, Honeytone for furniture that should look new, Goldtone if you want warmth, and Cleartone only if you accept more frequent recoats. Buy the matching two-part cleaner if your teak is grey, and plan for 48 hours of dry weather after cleaning.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are weighing it against alternatives, our <a href="/best-teak-sealer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best teak sealer guide</a> compares Semco with marine and furniture rivals, including longer-lasting options for owners who want fewer recoats. And if the wood you are finishing is not teak at all, the rules change; for example, a redwood deck wants a penetrating deck stain rather than a teak sealer, as our guide on <a href="/how-to-stain-a-redwood-deck/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how to stain a redwood deck</a> explains.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing teak that is not fully dry</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Semco's guide says the wood must be completely dry before application, and its distributor recommends 48 hours after cleaning. Sealer applied to damp teak cannot soak in, so it sits on the surface and weathers off unevenly. Wait for a dry window, especially after a two-part clean.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying too many coats</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The maker recommends two coats for the first application and warns that more will mask the grain. Over time, recoating too often causes build-up that can look blotchy and eventually peel. Check with a water splash first: if it still beads, the finish is working and does not need another coat.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pressure washing or using steel wool</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The care guide warns that pressure washers and brass brushes scour teak's soft grain, leaving it rough and splintery, and that steel wool leaves rust spots. Use the two-part cleaner with a Scotch-Brite pad or soft bristle brush, and rinse with a hose rather than a jet.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing over teak oil or varnish</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Semco needs bare, open wood. Teak oil residue and burnished oils stop it penetrating, and any intact varnish film must be stripped first, according to the maker. The two-part cleaner removes oil and old Semco, but not a solid coating. Strip, clean and dry before sealing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing Cleartone for full sun</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cleartone contains no pigment, and the maker says it is less resistant to the elements and needs more frequent recoating. On teak that sits in strong sun all day, a tinted shade such as Natural or Honeytone will hold colour noticeably longer between coats.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Semco teak sealer any good?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                By owner reputation, yes, for people who accept seasonal upkeep. It is easy to apply, leaves no lap marks and recoats without stripping. Its weakness is longevity on sun-baked horizontal teak, where owners commonly report recoating more than once a season. We have not tested it ourselves; this assessment comes from the maker's guidance and owner reports.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What colour is Semco teak sealer Natural?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The maker describes Natural as giving teak the golden-tan or sandy tan colour of slightly weathered teak, and calls it the favourite shade among boat owners. It is lighter and less red than Goldtone and less honey-coloured than Honeytone. Test it on a hidden spot first, because it reads differently on new and freshly cleaned teak.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does Semco teak sealer last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The maker and retailers describe it as season-long protection maintained by a single recoat at seasonal intervals. Owners report a wide range: some keep trim looking good for years with spring and autumn coats, while others in hot climates see fading within about six months. A water-bead test tells you when your teak is due.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Semco teak sealer water-based or oil-based?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sources disagree. The maker's care guide calls it solvent-based, and its 2016 safety data sheet lists mineral spirits as the main ingredient with 490 grams per litre of VOCs. Current US retailer listings describe it as water-based. It is not a teak oil in either case. Check the label and safety data sheet on the can you buy.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put Semco over teak oil?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not directly. The maker says Semco must go on clean, open wood, and teak oil residue prevents it from penetrating. Semco's two-part cleaner removes teak oils, food stains and old Semco, after which the wood needs to dry fully. Any solid film such as varnish has to be stripped before the cleaner is used.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can Semco be used on teak decks you walk on?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, above the waterline. Retailers list it as suitable for above the waterline only, and the maker says its main use is marine teak. Because it penetrates rather than forming a film, it does not create a glossy, slippery surface. High-traffic deck areas will need recoating more often than rails or furniture.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How does Star brite teak sealer compare with Semco?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Star brite markets its tinted teak sealer as a one-coat product for tropical sun and salt, applied after its own cleaner and brightener. Semco is designed around two thin same-day coats and seasonal touch-ups. Both are penetrating sealers rather than oils. We have not tested them side by side, so choose by shade and the routine you prefer.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
